### 2.1 Generative Watermarking

Generative watermarking applies the watermark while the LLM is
generating text. The LLM produces a probability distribution for the
next token, and the watermarking mechanism influences the sampling
process so that the generated sequence contains a detectable statistical
signal.

The paper describes three main components:

-   Random seed generator
-   Sampling algorithm
-   Scoring function

SynthID-Text uses **Tournament Sampling** as its sampling algorithm.

### 2.2 Edit-Based Watermarking

Edit-based watermarking modifies text after it has already been
generated. Examples mentioned in the paper include synonym substitution
and inserting special Unicode characters. Such methods can potentially
leave noticeable artefacts.

### 2.3 Data-Driven Watermarking

Data-driven watermarking changes the model's training data. The paper
describes training the LLM using specific trigger phrases. The model
produces watermarked outputs when prompted with those trigger phrases.
Its primary objective in the paper is identifying unauthorized misuse
rather than broadly attributing arbitrary generated text to an LLM.

