# 5. Tournament Sampling

## 5.1 Basic Idea

Tournament Sampling is the sampling algorithm introduced for
SynthID-Text.

The basic process is:

1.  Start with candidate tokens from the LLM probability distribution.
2.  Assign candidates pseudorandom `g`-values using the watermarking
    seed.
3.  Make candidates compete against each other.
4.  The candidate with the higher `g`-value wins a match.
5.  Continue through multiple tournament layers.
6.  The final winning token is selected.

``` text
Candidate Tokens
      |
      v
Competition
      |
      v
   Winners
      |
      v
Next Tournament Layer
      |
      v
   Winners
      |
      v
    ...
      |
      v
Final Winner
      |
      v
Output Token
```

## 5.2 `g`-Values

For each candidate token, a pseudorandom `g`-value is generated using
the watermarking seed. The `g`-values determine which candidate wins a
tournament match.

Example:

``` text
Token A -> g(A) = 0.73
Token B -> g(B) = 0.41

0.73 > 0.41

Winner = Token A
```

# 6. Tournament Layers

Tournament Sampling is performed through multiple layers. The paper
generally uses:

``` text
m = 30 layers
```

Simplified:

``` text
Layer 1 -> Competition -> Winners
Layer 2 -> Competition -> Winners
Layer 3 -> Competition -> Winners
...
Layer 30 -> Final competition -> Final winner
```

# 7. Non-Distortionary vs Distortionary Tournament Sampling

The paper distinguishes configurations according to the number of
competitors in each tournament match.

## 7.1 Non-Distortionary

``` text
Competitors per match = 2
```

Two candidate tokens compete in each match. The paper describes this as
non-distortionary at the token level.

``` text
Token A ----             >---- Winner
Token B ----/
```

## 7.2 Distortionary

``` text
Competitors per match > 2
```

More than two candidates can compete in a match. This creates a stronger
watermarking effect, but can introduce distortion into token selection
and potentially affect text quality.

``` text
Token A ----Token B -----Token C ------>---- Winner
Token D -----/
```

# 8. Why Tournament Sampling Is Useful

Tournament Sampling introduces watermark information during generation
rather than modifying completed text. The paper evaluates it while
balancing:

-   Detectability
-   Text quality
-   Computational cost
-   Scalability

